      "sources": ["Wikipedia, «Kusudama» — https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kusudama (a paper model usually made by sewing identical pyramidal units into a sphere; from kusuri, medicine, and tama, ball; used for incense and potpourri, possibly originally bunches of flowers or herbs; units may be glued together; early Japanese origami often used cutting and pasting; many modern kusudama are modular origami)", "David Mitchell, The Public Paperfolding History Project, «Kusudama» — https://origamiheaven.com/historyofkusudama.htm (kusudama were originally cloth bags of herbs; at an unknown date the word came to mean hanging balls of flowers folded from paper, often glued or sewn together; said to be one of the roots of modular origami, but historical confirmation is lacking)"]
